/-!
# Intersection multiplicity at a Cohen-Macaulay proper intersection

Let `A` be a regular local ring and let `I`, `J` be ideals of `A` such that the quotients
`A ⧸ I` and `A ⧸ J` are Cohen-Macaulay local rings, such that
`dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`, and such that `I ⊔ J` has the maximal ideal as its
radical. Then the intersection multiplicity of the pair computed by the Tor formula is the
length of `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`: all the higher Tor modules vanish and the alternating sum collapses
to its degree-zero term.

This is the local form, at the generic point of a component, of the geometric statement. For
`X` a nonsingular variety and `V`, `W` closed subvarieties intersecting properly, `Z` an
irreducible component of `V ∩ W` with generic point `ξ`, take `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` — a regular local
ring since `X` is nonsingular — and `I`, `J` the ideals cutting out `V` and `W` in `A`, so that
`A ⧸ I = 𝒪_{V, ξ}` and `A ⧸ J = 𝒪_{W, ξ}`. The two Cohen-Macaulay hypotheses are the source's;
the dimension inequality is proper intersection, since
`dim A - dim (A ⧸ I) - dim (A ⧸ J) = dim X + dim Z - dim V - dim W` vanishes exactly when `Z`
has the expected dimension, and the reverse inequality always holds; and the radical condition
says that `ξ` is the generic point of an irreducible component of `V ∩ W`, so that the trace of
`V ∩ W` on the local scheme at `ξ` is the closed point alone. The conclusion is
`e(X, V · W, Z) = length_{𝒪_{X, ξ}}(𝒪_{V ∩ W, ξ})`, the scheme-theoretic intersection
contributing `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`.

The proof is the source's. Tag 00O7, the form in which Auslander-Buchsbaum (Tag 090V) is
available over a regular local ring, supplies a resolution `F_• → A ⧸ I` by finite free modules
with `F_i = 0` for `i > dim A - depth (A ⧸ I)`, and Cohen-Macaulayness of `A` and of `A ⧸ I`
turns that bound into `dim A - dim (A ⧸ I)`, which proper intersection bounds by
`dim (A ⧸ J) = c`. Then `F_• ⊗_A (A ⧸ J)` computes `Tor_•^A(A ⧸ I, A ⧸ J)`, each of its terms
is a finite free `A ⧸ J`-module and so has depth `c` over `A`, and each of its homology modules
is annihilated by `I ⊔ J` and therefore supported at the maximal ideal alone. Were some homology
in positive degree nonzero, the acyclicity lemma (Tag 00N0) applied at the largest such degree
would give it depth at least `1`, whereas a nonzero finite module supported at the closed point
has depth `0`. So only `Tor_0^A(A ⧸ I, A ⧸ J) = A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` survives.

## Main results

* `Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_of_isCohenMacaulayLocalRing`: the multiplicity
  formula.
* `Ideal.tor_subsingleton_of_isCohenMacaulayLocalRing`: the vanishing of the higher Tor modules
  that it rests on.
* `Ideal.length_quotient_sup_ne_top_of_radical_eq_maximalIdeal`: the length on the right-hand
  side is finite, so that reading it as a natural number loses nothing.
* `Ideal.ringKrullDim_eq_add_of_isCohenMacaulayLocalRing`: under the same hypotheses the
  dimension inequality is an equality, which is the statement that the component has exactly
  the expected dimension.
* `Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_smul_of_isCohenMacaulayLocalRing`: the
  specialization in which `I` is generated by a regular sequence `f₁, …, f_c`, so that only
  `A ⧸ J` has to be assumed Cohen-Macaulay and the right-hand side is the length of
  `(A ⧸ J) ⧸ (f₁, …, f_c)(A ⧸ J)`.

## Implementation notes

The multiplicity is `Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity` (Tag 0AZR), the alternating sum
`∑ (-1)^i length_A Tor_i^A(A ⧸ I, M)` written as a `tsum` over `ℕ`; the vanishing result pins
every term above degree zero, so no truncation is chosen. Lengths are converted from `ℕ∞` to
`ℕ` to be summed in `ℚ`, which is legitimate here because the hypotheses make `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` of
finite length.

Proper intersection is the inequality `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)` rather than an
equality, matching Tag 0B1I: the reverse inequality holds for any pair of closed subvarieties,
so the inequality carries the whole hypothesis, and the equality is recovered as a conclusion.
Dimensions live in `WithBot ℕ∞`, which has no subtraction, so the dimension count is carried out
after extracting natural-number values, legitimate because a Noetherian local ring has finite
dimension.

Depth over `A` of an `A ⧸ J`-module is compared with depth over `A ⧸ J` by transporting regular
sequences along the surjection `A → A ⧸ J`, and the depth of a finite free module is read off
from the depth of one summand through the short exact sequences splitting off its factors.

The source's final sentence, that `V · W = [V ∩ W]_{dim V + dim W - dim X}` when `V` and `W` are
Cohen-Macaulay, is a statement about algebraic cycles and is carried in the sibling module
`CohenMacaulayCycle` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.ComputingIntersectionMultiplicities`
by `cycleAssociatedToClosedSubscheme_eq_intersectionProductCycle_of_isCohenMacaulay` in the
namespace `AlgebraicGeometry.TorFormula`, which applies the multiplicity formula below at each
point of the expected dimension.

Tag 0B06 is the specialization in which `V` is cut out at `ξ` by a regular sequence `f₁, …, f_c`,
the situation of Tag 0B04. Only `𝒪_{W, ξ}` has then to be assumed Cohen-Macaulay:
`𝒪_{V, ξ} = A ⧸ I` is the quotient of a regular local ring by a regular sequence and so is
Cohen-Macaulay automatically, and `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` is the same hypothesis as `I ⊔ J`
having the maximal ideal as its radical. That is the source's first proof of Tag 0B06; the
alternative deduction from Tag 0B04, through the quasi-regularity of `f₁, …, f_c` on `𝒪_{W, ξ}`
and the binomial form `(c + t choose c)` of the Hilbert function, is not formalized.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B02](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B02)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B06](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B06)
